-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Executive summary: no hardware ads, status quo continues.

I've finally finished the report on the poll on allowing hardware
advertisments in this group. The poll was posted about three weeks
ago, and I got 440 responses:

	complete no (no extra conditions): 140
	complete yes (no extra conditions): 232
	other (unclear, comments, special conditions): 68
	
The "no extra conditions" applies to the conditions outlined in the
earlier article (see the web archive for details).

If this had been a vote, the result would have been that hardware
ads would be allowed. However, as I told you, I'm more influenced
by good arguments than high numbers. Even some of those that were
counted for ads gave arguments why it would be a bad idea. The
argments that helped me make up my mind were:

	- Hardware is costly to transport, so almost all ads would
	  be of a local nature.
	  
	- Very few Intel-based systems are Linux-specific and making
	  decisions will be difficult and time-consuming.
	  
	- As soon as it becomes financially important to companies
	  that they get an advertisment posted to c.o.l.a, the
	  moderator will risk getting into fights. A really
	  unhappy company may even sue the moderator. (The
	  current commercial posts aren't too big a problem,
	  yet.)

	- Most companies who are willing to invest in Linux, are
	  going to advertise in Linux Journal anyway.

So, I'm sorry, but hardware advertisments will not be allowed.
However, announcements about new (versions of) drivers will
continue to be allowed, and are allowed to include (small)
advertisments. This is the current situation, so there will be
no change.

Many people had comments, questions, and suggestions. I don't
have the time to respond individually, or to all of them, but
here's the two most common suggestions:

* Make a new group or list or web page

	This is a good idea.  If anyone wants to start and RFD
	for a new group, called comp.os.linux.announce.hardware
	or something, I'll gladly support it. I won't have time
	to moderate it, but I doubt there will be a problem to
	find someone else.

* Advertisers should pay money for the priviledge

	This is a very, very tempting idea. However, it would
	mean that comp.os.linux.announce would become a business,
	and that is way too much work. It's also unclear whether
	the group would then be appropriate for the comp hierarchy.
	If I wanted to start demanding money for ads, I'd start a
	mailing list or web page instead.
	
	I already get CD-ROM's from some companies, at least
	InfoMagic, Red Hat, Caldera, S.u.S.E, Walnut Creek,
	and Yggdrasil. Possibly more, sorry if I forgot
	anyone.  I have more CD's than I know what to do with,
	since I don't have enough computers to try them all
	out. Actually, I've lent most of them (but not those
	with commercial software) to a student association that
	lends them to its members.
	
	I also get the Linux Journal for free (though I haven't
	seen any new issues for a few weeks). I've got a package
	with Applixware (which I haven't tried -- I don't run
	Red Hat and haven't got the time to install RH, try AW,
	and re-install Debian). I've got several variants of the
	books with LDP materials (Linux Bible, Dr Linux), and a
	couple others.

	I'm happy.[1]

- -- 
Read <<http://www.iki.fi/liw/mail-to-lasu.html> before you mail me.
Please don't Cc public replies to me.

[1] Even though I didn't get the $1M and the Alpha. :-)

