-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I have just uploaded Yard 1.7 to Sunsite.  Yard is a suite of Perl scripts
for creating a so-called rescue disk (a floppy disk comprising a kernel and
a filesystem with useful rescue programs).  Rescue disks are used when you
can't (or don't want to) boot off your hard disk; they usually contain
utilities for diagnosing and manipulating hard disks and filesystems.

Yard lets you specify a set of files to be included on the rescue
disk. Yard's specifications are flexible.  The primary feature of Yard is
that it does some checking of your choices to make sure that the rescue
disk will be usable.  Yard checks for basic mistakes that I found myself
making repeatedly: missing/unlinked libraries, unresolved symbolic links,
typos, etc.  These mistakes are tedious to search for and many of them can
be detected automatically, which is why I wrote Yard.

Its location at the moment is :
	ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/Incoming/yard-1.7.tar.gz

Its eventual home will be in /pub/Linux/system/Recovery.



NEW WITH 1.7:
Bugs fixed:
	Yard assumed "x => y" from ldd meant that y is a real file, which
		isn't always the case.
	A few configuration variables were not in the CFG package.
	"/dev/ram" was hardwired in create_replacements; now checks
		for different ramdisk device names.
Added test for cp 3.13 bug, which causes problems for Yard.  Also
	included source patch in extras directory.
	(Thanks to Horst von Brand).
Docs reorganized and SGMLified using linuxdoc-sgml package.  The docs
	directory contains ASCII and Postscript versions plus the SGML source.






