-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


Dear Sirs and Madams of the Board Of Directors,

Considering the huge public interest in an obscure operating system known
amongst insiders as "Linux", I would like to bring the following topic under
your attention.

Most users of the aforementionned operating system tend to use their system
in either a graphical mode ("X" is what they call it, probably because they
don't know what it is) or a textual mode.

The former mode has already been covered extensively by a competing company
called "XFree86 Inc." and several others. Oddly enough, XFree86 Inc. is
distributing their software for free. Even more suprisingly, they are the
market leaders!

The latter operating mode, "text mode", also referred to as "console", seems
not to be covered by any product on the market.

In view of the above, I would strongly suggest that our company enters this
lucrative market with a product of its own. Yours truly is aware of the
growing popularity of graphical user interfaces, as provided by XFree86 Inc.
and others. Still, many users still stick to text modes for its speed and
low resource usage.

I have made up an example of what such a product could look like (no, don't
worry, I did not do this on office hours -- no need to fire me -- yet), and
have created a standard company product description in the well-known LSM
format for your information:

Begin3
Title: SVGA textmode manipulation/enhancement
Version: 1.4
Entered-date: 22SEP96
Description: Uses extra features found on SVGA video cards (high pixel
             clocks) to enhance Linux text modes, independently of what the
             BIOS can do. It is configured with an XF86Config-like file, and
             allows setting of pixel clock, H/V timings, font size, cursor
             size, etc.
             Enables using the video card AND the monitor to their full
             potential in textmode, as with the XFree86 X-server.
             Current support includes S3, ET3000, ET4000, ET6000, Cirrus
             Logic, Trident, Western Digital, ATI (and MACH32), Video7
             (Headland), Avance Logic (ALI), Oak Technology (OTI), SiS,
             RealTek, ARK, NCR, Genoa (GVGA) and MX cards.
             It can also use an external clock program and supports all S3
             clock chips from XFREE 3.1.2. Features automatic font loading,
             monitor definition (H/V frequency limits), on-the-fly screen
             resizing, cursor shape definition, ...
             Also includes: palette changer, pixelclock probe, text/graphics
             mode grabber, VGA register hacking tool, and a multi-chipset/
             clockchip ClockProg for X that supports ALL chipsets and
             clockchips from SVGATextMode.
             Binaries for Linux-ELF and DOS included for SVGATextMode and
             grabmode.
Keywords: SVGA VGA console screen monitor XFREE Xconfig XF86Config
Author: koen.gadeyne@barco.com (Koen Gadeyne)
Maintained-by: koen.gadeyne@barco.com (Koen Gadeyne)
Primary-site: tsx-11.mit.edu pub/linux/sources/sbin
              301k SVGATextMode-1.4-src.tar.gz
              208k SVGATextMode-1.4-bin.tar.gz
              240k SVGATextMode-1.4-dos.tar.gz
Alternate-site: sunsite.unc.edu pub/Linux/utils/console
Original-site:
Platforms: Linux machine (Intel or Alpha) with one of the supported VGA
           cards, kernel 1.1.54 or newer.
Copying-policy: GNU General Public License (GPL) version 2
End


Since the version number suggests there have been previous versions of this
program, I will include a short list of changes to what could be called "the
elder version" (version 1.3):

- - setpalette now tells the kernel about the new color palette, so on kernels
  >1.3.3 switching VT's doesn't restore the old color palette, but remembers
  the new one.

- - S3Trio memory clock programming support.

- - fixed problem with S3Trio support for standard VGA clocks.

- - ET6000 support!

- - updated ET4000 clock selection code to new XFree86 3.1.2C+ method.
  This obsoletes the "AltClockSel" method.

- - PRELIMINARY S3 Virge support. UNTESTED. Somebody wanne give me one?



Please also note that previous versions consisted of one single tar.gz file,
which has now been split up into three files:

    SVGATextMode-1.4-src.tar.gz : source-only distribution (no binaries --
                                  not even for DOS). for those with an
                                  installed C-compiler, this is the thing to
                                  get.

    SVGATextMode-1.4-bin.tar.gz : (Linux-) binaries only. Includes
                                  all required stuff for installation
                                  (manuals, documentation,...)

    SVGATextMode-1.4-dos.tar.gz : DOS binaries of SVGATextMode and grabmode.
                                  included Windows grabmode.


The program may be available on the following sites, and their mirrors:

ftp://tsx-11.mit.edu/pub/linux/sources/sbin/SVGATextMode-1.4*
ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/utils/console/SVGATextMode-1.4*
ftp://ansc.une.edu.au/pub/SVGATextMode/SVGATextMode-1.4*
http://www.ccc.nottingham.ac.uk/~etzpc/stm.html

If the files are not in place yet, check the "incoming" directories first!


To end my plea, I would also want to suggest that We also distribute this
product for free, as to assure a similar market leadership as with the
graphical user interface companies. And that we remain doing so until hell
freezes up.


Yours humbly,

Koen Gadeyne.


