Calling all MkLinux fans...

It appears that MkLinux is beginning to develop into a community of its
own. That in mind, I would like to propose the first MkLinux distribution:
Project Andromeda.

Andromeda will be designed as a two-tiered OS similar to Windows NT, with
a default installation for workstations and another for servers. I hope to
make it available in a shippable form by the end of the year, possibly in
time to be released with the final version of MkLinux for Power Macintosh.
The objectives of Andromeda are as follows:

	-To provide a stable and usable server OS for any environment on the Macintosh
	-To provide a productivity platform that echoes the work Caldera is doing 
	-To provide cost-effective mainframe migration services for those
operations who may be affected by the upcoming Year 2000 crisis
	-To provide a platform for new innovations in the Unix world

My hope is to be able to provide all of this in a freeware platform. To
this end, I'm looking for the following:

	-protocol stacks for Netware, NetBEUI, and SNA (Appletalk and TCP/IP
already exist)
	-freeware productivity software (word processors, spreadsheets, etc.)
	-a good, stable freeware database package
	-user-friendly sysadmin and user tools (of the DOS Shell/Finder/SCO Shell ilks)
	-decent user documentation
	-donations of a Jaz drive and a used PowerMac to make this project a
reality much sooner

Please contact me at connorbd@cleo.bc.edu if you're interested. Also,
within the next month or so I will put up a Web page on the Andromeda
project; look for it at http://www.cs.bc.edu/~connorbd/andromeda.html,
where I will post progress reports, feature lists, and hopefully a link to
the distribution when it's finished. 

Brian Connors
President
Duchesne Brick and Block component software


