CLUE Newsletter, September 1996
===============================

This Month's Topic: 

   "What Microsoft and IBM Don't Want to Talk About - How volunteer 
    team work on the Internet built something better." 

   Randy Bentson, author of "Inside Linux" (published by SSC)
   will be in town and will speak at the September CLUE meeting!
   Randy is being sponsored by SSC and WGS and will also be
   appearing on Todd Stauffer's Peak Computing Magazine radio show
   (date and time to be announced at the CLUE meeting).  We are 
   working to arrange a book signing as well.  CLUE welcomes and 
   encourages other User Groups in the area who might have an 
   interest in this meeting to attend.  Please contact 
   President@CLUE.Denver.CO.US or mjhammel@csn.net for more information.

   Anyone wishing to be involved in sponsoring Randy Bentson's visit
   or other future visits from other Linux personalities please contact 
   the CLUE President at President@CLUE.Denver.CO.US.

   Meeting Details:
   -------------------------------------------------------------------
   When:            7pm, Thursday, September 19, 1996

   Where:           Smoky Hill High School, near Parker Road & Quincy in
                    Aurora.

   Who:             Randy Bentson, author of Inside Linux - A Look at
                    Operating System Development.
   More
   Information:     http://www.clue.denver.co.us/randyb.html
   Contact Person:  Eric Gustafson <President@CLUE.Denver.CO.US>
   Cost:            FREE
   Sponsored by:    Colorado Linux Users and Enthusiasts, (CLUE)
                    Smoky Hill High School
                    Specialized System Consultants, Inc. (SSC)
                    WorkGroup Solutions (WGS, Inc.)


Next Month's Topic:
   Jeremy Chatfield from X Inside will speak about CDE, the
   Common Desktop Environment.  Based on X11R5 and Motif 1.2,
   CDE is an attempt by a number of companies to provide a 
   common graphical interface for the Unix desktop, including system
   administration and printing.

   Jeremy Chatfield has a long history with Unix systems, 
   including managing the Dell Unix group back in the early 90's.

Future Events:
   Keep an eye out for our Linux Installation Workshops, to be
   announced soon!

Member announcments:
   Kenneth Geisshirt, a student at UC Boulder, has expressed an 
   interest in coming to the September meeting but doesn't have any
   way to get down to Smokey Hill High School.  If anyone from the
   Boulder area is coming to the meeting in September and could
   offer Kenneth a lift, would you please contact him?  He can be
   reached at kneth@lord.colorado.edu or 303-492-6491.

===========================

Notes from August's meeting:
   "How to keep prying eyes off your data: An Intro to data
   encryption with a focus on PGP." -- By Mike Miller

      Why do you need PGP?

      It's personal. It's private. And it's no one's business but yours.
      You may be planning a political campaign, discussing your taxes, or
      having an illicit affair. Or you may be doing something that you 
      feel shouldn't be illegal, but is. Whatever it is, you don't want 
      your private electronic mail (E-mail) or confidential documents 
      read by anyone else. There's nothing wrong with asserting your 
      privacy. Privacy is as apple-pie as the Constitution. 
         - Philip Zimmerman

   Mike Miller gave us a brief history of encryption and secure
   communication down through the ages., including a discusion of 
   the "Lil' Orphan Annie" decoder ring and the Enigma machine
   (see http://www.datacomm.com/~troll/lect09.html).  He also provided
   an enlightening discussion on the mathematics behind few of
   the encryption methods.

   Check out the CLUE home page at http://www.clue.denver.co.us/~clue/
   to see a summary of Mike's talk.


   X Inside is hiring!
   -------------------

   The guys from X Inside stopped by to mention that they're
   hiring!  There are a number of positions open, some entry level,
   some with more experience required.  X Inside develops and sells
   various components for X Windows environments on multiple Unix
   platforms.  You can contact them at http://www.xinside.com or
   try sales@xinside.com.  X Inside is located in Denver, Colorado.


   Linux Gazette is looking for writers!
   -------------------------------------

   Marjorie Richardson at SSC is now the managing editor for the
   Linux Gazette.  Majorie has been working with Michael Hammel
   (Clue Newsletter writer) on his article for Linux Journal (see
   the Novemember Issue of Linux Journal) and asked that he spread
   the word that she's looking for articles for the Linux Gazette.
   This is a free, online magazine.  If you've got an idea for an
   article, a neat trick or tip for Linux, or just some useful
   configuration of a popular tool, send email to Marjorie!  You can
   check out the Linux Gazette at http://www.ssc.com/lg/index.html
   or send email to gazette@ssc.com.
   
===========================

Administrivia:

CLUE is interested in organizing a booth at local trade shows.  Would
   you be interested in this?  Would you participate?  Come to the September
   meeting and let us know your thoughts on such local Linux exposure.

CLUE welcomes members of the Linux User Community to speak at the monthly
   meetings.  Please contact President@CLUE.Denver.CO.US to arrange
   your talk.  All speakers are expected to offer a written summary of
