Essentia: Product Description

- - General Overview

InterSoft Essentia is a database server developed with a new revolutionary 
technology: RISE.

While other databases compete in size and complexity, Essentia puts in the 
first place simplicity, utility and power.

- - The Technology

RISE (Reduced Instruction Set Engine) is a concept analog to that of the RISC 
technology for microprocessors. The basic idea is to have a process (the 
engine) that provides a number of services (hence the name server) to other 
processes called clients. Some examples of servers are: window managers, 
communication servers, etc.

The communication between the server and the clients is performed through 
a perfectly defined interface, but of small size. This small number of 
messages allows, however, to define functions that perform extremely 
complex tasks, without loosing simplicity or speed.

- - Some other features:

	- Automatic consistency check: each time the server starts, it verifies 
	the transaction state. Thus, the database is held consistent until the 
	last complete transaction. To acomplish this, Essentia uses a mechanism 
	of checkpoints and journals.           
	
	- Database versions: this mechanism --inexistent in other traditional 
	DBMS-- allows to mantain the state of any database to a given date. 
	In this way, a process can ``see'' how the database ``looked'' at a 
	given date while other is entering current data, without duplicating 
	size or information.
	
	- Incremental backup: when it is necessary to backup the database 
	contents, Essentia allows to perform incremental backups, i.e., saving 
	only the modification since the last backup.. This task is performed 
	by a client process, not by the server, outdating not only old ``full 
	data'' backups but also allowing users to continue acessing the data 
	while it is being copied.
	
	- Mirroring: Essentia allows to maintain an instantaneous image of the 
	database, which is parallel to the real database. Thus, if a server 
	dies, another may continue working with the data image.
	
	- Shadowing: is a technique to handle the data-modification-creation 
	process with security. First, the information that is to be modified 
	is copied (to the shadow), the user works with that copy and at 
	checkpoint-time the changes are updated to disk.
	
	- Two Phase Comit: This tecnique is used to avoid inconsistencies when
	a transaction involves several servers. When a client executes an 
	EndTransaction, and this transaction involves several servers, a 
	message is sent to all involved servers, asking if they are ready 
	to end the transaction (ReadyToEnd). The server verifies the conditions
	to end the transaction. If it is possible, the server answers "yes",
	on the contrary, the server answers "AbortTransaction" telling the 
	client that the transaction could not be finished. For the last, 
	the message "ReadyToEnd" is sent to the "Primary Server" which is the 
	server who decides whether the transaction has been made or not. If 
	any of the involved servers suffers a failure, it will ask the 
	"Primary Server" if the transaction was aborted or not, as it comes 
	up again.
	
- - Other Products:
	
	- Iqlsrv (InterSoft SQL Server), used as an interface between 
	(Intersoft's) ODBC implementation, JDBC and Essentia data base engine.
	- ODBC (InterSoft Essentia-ODBC driver).
	- JDBC (InterSoft Essentia-JDBC driver).

- - Note:
	The following products are released as free software with no restrictions 
	whatsoever:
	- Essentia
	- iqlsrv
	- ODBC
	- JDBC

