Wrox Press are proud to announce their new book:

     Beginning Linux Programming

     By Neil Matthew and Richard Stones
     Available now

     UNIX Programming step-by-step with graduated and realistic example code
     Network programming in C
     Script writing with bash and Tcl
     X Windows with Tk and Java
     Internet CGI and HTML


You've installed Linux, or have access to a version of UNIX. You're happy
with your environment and its configuration, and you're not (too) scared of
using the system. You know the basics of C (or have a handy reference), and
you want to program for UNIX. What else do you need?

This book takes off where most Linux books stop, showing you how to make the
most of the tools UNIX offers to program UNIX for real. The book is also
unique in that it teaches UNIX programming in a simple and structured way,
using Linux and its associated and freely available development tools as the
main platform.

The book aims to discuss UNIX programming as described in the relevant POSIX
and X/Open specifications, so the code is tested with that in mind. All the
source code from the book is available under the terms of the Gnu Public
License from the Wrox web site.

