FALL '96 COMDEX FEATURES LINUX TECHNOLOGY SHOWCASE

AMHERST, NH -- September 5 --  Linux International is pleased to announce
that a Linux Technology Showcase will be featured at the annual Fall '96
Comdex this November 18-22nd in Las Vegas, downstairs at the Sands
Convention Hall, starting at booth number C8118.

The showcase highlights the robust, high-quality 32-bit GUI, multiuser,
multitasking and multimedia operating system that has gained loyal users
among businesses, educational institutions, governmental agencies, and PC
enthusiasts worldwide.  This year, Linux International and a host of
companies including Digital Equipment Corporation, WorkGroup Solutions,
Linux Journal/SSC, and Red Hat Software will be exhibiting the latest
advances in Linux, applications and related products.

Linux International's mission is to promote and aid in the further
development of Linux.  It is a non-profit organization run by a corps
of volunteers, many of whom are high-profile Linux developers and
activists.  The organization serves as a bridge between the dedicated
and skilled community of developers and the world of commerce and industry.

Linux is a viable and low-cost PC alternative to DOS- and Windows-based
operating systems or network servers.  It includes the standard Unix
graphical interface, the X-Windows System, supports both 32- and 64-bit
platforms and provides high performance networking with configurations
that are otherwise impossible for NT or DOS-based servers.

In recent months, a number of business applications have been created for
Linux and marketed by several commercial developers.  These include
database, spreadsheet, word processing, graphics, and more.  Well-known
applications like WordPerfect and Netscape now have versions of their
software ported to Linux.  Furthermore, the existence of DOS and Macintosh
emulations (a Windows emulation is on the way) allows Linux to run much of
today's popular MS-DOS and Mac software.

Also known for its flexible Internet connectivity, Linux comes with its own
web server software, programming tools, E-mail and other utilities.  In
fact, more than half of all Internet Service Provider (ISP) sites set up
last year were Linux-based; ISP managers have found the combination of
Linux' price, power, and flexibility well-suited to their growing businesses.

Because of the high demand for skilled Unix programmers, Linux is also an
ideal way for novices to learn Unix systems concepts and programming.
Developers have a complete Unix development workstation with Linux, one
that includes the extensive programming tools and languages for a fraction
of the cost of other Unix systems.  Furthermore, everything they create may
be distributed royalty-free.

Anyone interested in learning more about Linux, or anyone looking for a more
powerful, flexible OS or Internet/Intranet server solution is invited to
visit the Linux Technology Showcase and witness the many benefits of Linux
firsthand.


Linux International

Linux International was established to promote the development and use
of Linux.  The people at Linux International know how good Linux is and
want it to become an accepted competitor to products from even the largest
computer companies.  Among other things, it serves as a bridge between the
dedicated and skilled community of developers, and the general world of
commerce and industry.

Linux International is a non-profit organization run by volunteers, many
of whom are high-profile Linux developers or activists.

For more information, please visit Linux International's World Wide Web
site at http://www.li.org or contact li@li.org.

