Hi,

A new version of CLISP, a Common Lisp implementation, is on sunsite.

In the 1994 release, it already had an interpreter, a compiler which
produces compact bytecode, the CLOS object system, an X11 interface (CLX)
and uses the readline library.

In the 1996 release, it now has
 * a foreign function interface,
 * a socket interface (thanks to Marcus Daniels),
 * add-on C modules, e.g. regular expressions, Linux libc call interface,
 * readline library: parenthesis matching,
 * generational garbage collection also for Linux,
 * some bug fixes, especially in the LOOP macro.

There are pre-built binaries on sunsite. Use CLISP if you can't get GCL
compiled.

CLISP is small, whereas CMUCL is big and compiles to machine code. So
use CLISP it if you don't have much memory, but use CMUCL [afterwards]
if you want your programs to perform as well as C programs.


                Bruno Haible
                <haible@ma2s2.mathematik.uni-karlsruhe.de>


