Subject: bzip 0.21, a statistical data compressor
Date: 1996/09/09
Message-ID: <cola-liw-842256895-21576-0@liw.clinet.fi>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 179419413
organization: ?
followup-to: comp.os.linux.misc
newsgroups: comp.os.linux.announce


-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

bzip is a lossless statistical data compressor.  It uses the recent
Burrows-Wheeler block-sorting transformation to compress files to
within about 5% of leading edge PPM techniques.

bzip compresses and decompresses more slowly than GNU gzip, and
requires a lot more memory, but is still fast enough and small enough
to be genuinely useful to those needing maximum compression.
Here are some numbers typifying what kind of compression
improvments you can get (.gz is GNU Gzip, .bz is bzip):

   -rw-rw-r--  1 sewardj  28211200 Sep  6 14:02 gcc-2.7.2.tar
   -rw-rw-r--  1 sewardj   7089723 Sep  6 14:02 gcc-2.7.2.tar.gz
   -rw-rw-r--  1 sewardj   5243773 Sep  6 14:02 gcc-2.7.2.tar.bz

The command-line interface is very similar to that of gzip, so you can
use bzip as a drop-in replacement for gzip, if you like.  For more
information, and the distribution, point your browser at:

   http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/arch/people/j-seward/index.html

Because of possible patent infringement problems, you should
not use bzip for commercial purposes.

Julian Seward (sewardj@cs.man.ac.uk)


